WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:12.190
Life in the microcosmos is tough for its unicellular
residents.

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:19.019
Prokaryotes and protists have to compete for
food and space while also trying to avoid

00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:25.740
organisms that are more than happy to see
all of their complex, energy-filled biological

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:33.280
bits as an afternoon snack, and managing those
tasks can be even more daunting when you are

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.320
very, very small.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:42.340
So instead of facing all these challenges
alone, many of these organisms treat survival

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:45.150
as a team effort.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:52.710
As cells divide, they adhere to one another,
building a colony that becomes larger and

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:55.840
larger with every cell division.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:04.170
An individual cell can only get so big before
its size comes at the expense of other abilities.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:10.920
But a colony can get much bigger, providing
protection against predators and a competitive

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:14.250
advantage when it comes to finding space and
food.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:22.490
And as the cells come together and organize,
a colony can enable organisms to do so much

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:24.900
more than they could on their own.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:30.960
It’s an advantage that became a vital step
on the path not just to more complexity in

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:38.940
the microcosmos, but also a vital step on
the path to us.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:47.640
But before we get to that, let’s check out
this long green thing cutting across the screen.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:52.590
Maybe it looks like a blade of grass, rudely
floating in everybody’s way.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:59.740
But if you look closer, you can see thin filaments
made up of individual cells of a freshwater

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:04.000
cyanobacteria called Aphanizomenon.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.769
Often times the names of these organisms are
really a pain to say, but Aphanizomenon is

00:02:10.769 --> 00:02:13.150
just a joy.

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:19.090
If these cells were on their own, they’d
basically be little balls of candy just waiting

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:25.250
to get chomped but these long grass-like structures
make it harder for filter feeders like water

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:29.000
flea to eat the Aphanizomenon colony.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.460
In lakes and ponds, these grassy structures can

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:38.740
accumulate, creating dense, visible blooms that release
toxins into the water.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:50.200
Nostoc colonies are also made up of filaments,
like a string of beads, where the beads are

00:02:50.209 --> 00:02:52.349
cyanobacteria.

00:02:52.349 --> 00:02:59.090
But where Aphanizomenon likes to keep its
strands elongated, Nostoc wraps its filaments

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:04.310
around a structure made mostly of different
types of sugar that it excretes to create

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:08.250
a round, gelatinous mass.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:13.830
These colonies vary in size, but some particularly
large ones have been measured with a diameter

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:16.870
of 22 cm.

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:23.720
I realize I said that like 22cm is very big,
but it is when you’re talking about a colony

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:26.870
of single-celled organisms.

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:29.860
Nostoc use sunlight to make their food.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:33.680
But in the process, they also produce oxygen.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:40.890
This is a problem because Nostoc also likes
to fix nitrogen from the air to make necessary

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:46.340
chemicals , and oxygen interferes with a key
step in that process.

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:53.190
But living in a colony means that some of
the Nostoc cells can specialize, developing

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:58.940
a thicker exterior that prevents oxygen from
getting inside the cell.

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:05.160
These specialized cells are called heterocysts,
and they can focus on fixing nitrogen without

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:14.110
any worries of intruding oxygen, while the
rest of the colony can continue photosynthesizing.

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:17.320
Bacteria aren’t the only species that form
colonies.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:22.100
These spiked, circular creatures are Pseudopediastrum
boryanum.

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:29.760
The colonies are flat, and the cells are arranged
in concentric circles around each other.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:34.940
Where the Aphanizomenon and Nostoc colonies
we showed earlier seemed to be full of cells

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:43.910
strung together, the Pseudopediastrum colonies
are usually made up of 8,16, or 32 cells,

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:50.270
whose walls contain sporopollenin, a hardy
material that protects the cells from the

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:51.760
environment.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:58.130
The gold algae Synura also forms colonies,
with individual cells coming together like

00:04:58.130 --> 00:05:00.389
little yellow marigolds.

00:05:00.389 --> 00:05:06.889
Each cell is encased in silica scales and
has two whip-like structures called flagella,

00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:10.120
which tumble the colony through water.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:16.810
When Synuras bloom, they can turn a whole
pond yellow, letting off a smell that is somewhat

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:22.130
hard to describe, but even if you can’t
see the pond, you’ll definitely be able

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:26.389
to smell it.

00:05:26.389 --> 00:05:32.560
This Gonium colony is made up of several flagellated
cells arranged in a sphere.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:37.081
It may look simpler compared to some of the
other colonies we’ve seen, but with the

00:05:37.081 --> 00:05:44.610
rest of its volvocine algae relatives, it
may hold answers to how multicellular life

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:46.380
evolved.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:52.470
Members of this genus are either unicellular
or form colonies that, through evolution,

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:58.600
have increased in complexity from species
to species, like this Eudorina colony, whose

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:03.330
structure is more complex when compared to
its predecessors.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:08.919
Like Gonium, the Eudorina colony is made up
of only one type of cell, repeated around

00:06:08.919 --> 00:06:10.620
its surface.

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:16.930
But these colonies created the structure that
would serve as the evolutionary stepping stone

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:27.169
for a species just on the edge of multicellular,
Pleodorina, which contains specialized reproductive

00:06:27.169 --> 00:06:33.720
cells, and then ultimately to the definitely
multi-cellular Volvox algae.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:40.000
Like the colonies before it, Volvox is built
around a gooey transparent matrix.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:46.350
And like Pleodorina, it contains a set of
specialized reproductive cells, just a tad

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:48.550
bit more specialized.

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:53.400
Those smaller green spots are somatic cells,
or body cells.

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:59.010
they don’t reproduce or divide, and one
of their main jobs is to help move the colony

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:00.180
using their flagella.

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:05.319
But after a few days, when their duty is done,
those cells die.

00:07:05.319 --> 00:07:12.449
Meanwhile, on the inside are the larger gonidia
cells, which drive asexual reproduction and

00:07:12.449 --> 00:07:16.090
might be immortal.

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:19.120
Now remember the Nostoc colonies that we talked
about before?

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:24.590
They also had elements of specialization,
with some cells devoted to nitrogen fixing

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:26.660
and others devoted to photosynthesis.

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:34.780
The difference between Nostoc and Volvox however,
is that Volvox reproduces by creating multi-cellular

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:37.870
daughters inside of it.

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:44.699
The gonidia cells divide creating a kind of
baby volvox with all of the somatic cells

00:07:44.699 --> 00:07:48.060
and gonidia cells it will need as it matures.

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:56.410
It’s an organism inside an organism, and
when they’re released, it is not cell division,

00:07:56.410 --> 00:07:59.680
it’s is birth.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:06.150
Another example of how, when you look very
very close, every sharp line turns out to

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:07.419
be blurry.

00:08:07.419 --> 00:08:14.599
The colonies that assemble in the microcosmos
are important, not just for the survival of

00:08:14.599 --> 00:08:21.699
the organisms that comprise them, but to us and our own understanding of how our own multicellular

00:08:21.699 --> 00:08:24.039
existence came into being.

00:08:24.039 --> 00:08:30.430
Whether bacteria or algae, the way these colonies
come together and the shapes they take on

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:35.519
can be as distinctive as the individual organisms
they’re made of.

00:08:35.519 --> 00:08:41.929
They make life in the microcosmos just that
much more complex, forming the foundations

00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:47.560
for multicellular life both under the microscope and beyond.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:49.980
Thank you for coming on this journey with us.

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:53.380
If you want to see more from our Master of Microscopes, James

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:56.700
check out Jam and Germs on Instagram.

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:00.960
And if you want a new video like this in your subscription box every week,

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:04.240
there's gotta be a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

